{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey security deposit law — owner liability on sale and withholding penalties","requirement":"After a sale, the new owner must obtain the tenant's deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving notices and returning it. Willfully withholding a deposit from a tenant receiving state or federal aid carries a $500 to $2,000 penalty per offense.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 civil penalty per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenancies in residential rental property in New Jersey; the withholding penalty applies where the tenant receives state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential rental units; no building-age or unit-count limits stated"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Willful withholding from an assisted tenant: civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 per offense, collected by summary proceeding. Unlawful use of deposit monies: disorderly person, fine of $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Law Against Discrimination source-of-lawful-income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in housing based on a person's source of lawful income, along with the other protected characteristics listed in the Law Against Discrimination.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to all landlord-tenant relationships except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned for and occupied by one sex.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"All landlord-tenant relationships except stated owner-occupied and single-sex housing"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, i.e. YMCA and age-restricted housing as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD are subject to fines and penalties up to $10,000 for a first offense; monetary damages and an order to rent the unit to the complainant may be required.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a dwelling used as a residence only by tenant consent or through the legal eviction process, must have good cause, and must prove the grounds in court. Residential landlords may not fail to renew a lease without good cause.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; each cause except nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in a written Notice to Quit, and a Notice to Cease may also be required.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Dwelling units used as a residence; landlord must prove grounds in court"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"New Jersey has no statewide rent control law; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances. Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from local rent control for 30 years after construction is completed.","key_value":"30 years following completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in New Jersey; other residential rentals are covered only as a municipality's rent control or rent leveling ordinance provides.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rent control set by municipal ordinances; new construction exempt for 30 years after completion"},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings for 30 years after completion of construction; rents subsidized by governmental funding; HUD-owned, HUD-subsidized or HUD-insured projects with economic problems; and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law ( N.J.S.A. 2A:42 -84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"State law leaves rent control to municipalities; local rent control or rent leveling ordinances govern where adopted.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and unconscionability standard","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease: the old lease must be terminated and a new lease offered at the increased rent, with written notice that the tenant may remain by paying it. An increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; before evicting for nonpayment of an increased rent the landlord must serve a valid notice to quit and notice of rent increase.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"Municipal rent control ordinances govern where they exist; where none exists a judge decides whether the increase is unconscionable.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rent may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion; increases for new services or amenities are still allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, regardless of whether they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"The prohibition does not prevent rents from being increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
